module bindings.javascript.find_my_way_path_match_normalization;

// find-my-way is the router Fastify dispatches with, and it does not match a
// request path verbatim. Four documented options change the string it matches:
// `ignoreDuplicateSlashes` collapses `//` runs, `ignoreTrailingSlash` drops a
// final `/`, `caseSensitive: false` lower-cases the path, and
// `useSemicolonDelimiter` cuts the path at the first `;`. Percent-decoding is
// the last step, reached through `safeDecodeURI(url, useSemicolonDelimiter)`
// exported from `find-my-way/lib/url-sanitizer` — the decoder alone, with the
// delimiter decision passed in.
//
// Anything that decides for itself whether a request belongs to a registered
// route has to reproduce that whole pipeline, because the router's answer is
// what actually runs. Connect-style middleware engines do exactly this: they
// compile each registered path to a regular expression and test the incoming
// URL against it to decide whether the middleware runs or is skipped. An engine
// that calls `safeDecodeURI` and stops has implemented only the last step, so it
// disagrees with the router on every path the four options normalize —
// `/a//b`, `/a/b/`, `/A/B` and `/a/b;x=1` are all dispatched to the handler
// registered at `/a/b` while the middleware's regular expression declines them.
//
// The consequence is not a failed request. The middleware is silently skipped
// and the handler runs unguarded, so an authentication, authorization, CSRF or
// rate-limiting middleware registered for a route is bypassed by respelling the
// path. No credential and no application bug are involved: the attacker is any
// client that can reach the route.
//
// So the binding reads three facts out of the matching function and negates a
// fourth:
//
//   * `call_path:safeDecodeURI` — the function canonicalizes with find-my-way's
//     decoder, which is what marks it as reproducing the router's view of the
//     path rather than parsing a URL for some other purpose;
//   * `call:exec` or `call:test` — and tests the result against a regular
//     expression, which is the dispatch decision;
//   * a `req.url` / `req.originalUrl` selector — on the request's own path;
//   * and no read of `ignoreDuplicateSlashes`, `ignoreTrailingSlash`,
//     `caseSensitive` or `useSemicolonDelimiter`, the four options that decide
//     what the router matched.
//
// The negation names the router's documented options, not any particular
// remediation. Both routes out of the defect satisfy it: normalizing inline
// before the decode leaves the option reads in the matching function, and
// moving the whole canonicalization into a helper takes `safeDecodeURI` out of
// it, so the positive conjunct no longer holds. Neither a helper's name nor the
// property the configuration is read from is named here, because those differ
// between the two engines that carry this shape.
//
// The option names are read from `selector:` tokens, which are member accesses
// derived from the AST, so a comment naming an option cannot stand in for a
// read of it.
//
// One cost, inherent to the way a JavaScript function context inherits the
// tokens of the functions nested inside it: an engine whose matching closure
// sits inside a factory function reports on each enclosing scope as well as on
// the closure itself. The extra rows point at the same defect.
binding fmwPathMatchNormalization {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.callPath == "safeDecodeURI"
    and node.context.call in ["exec", "test"]
    and containsAny(node.context.selector, ["req.originalUrl", "req.url", "request.originalUrl", "request.url"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.selector, ["ignoreDuplicateSlashes", "ignoreTrailingSlash", "caseSensitive", "useSemicolonDelimiter"])
  emit issue code.MiddlewarePathMatchNormalizationMismatch at node
}
